<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palatine Chapel, Palermo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1132–1140</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Cappella Palatina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Palatine Chapel, or Cappella Palatina, is the royal chapel of the Norman palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, built for King Roger II from 1132 and consecrated in 1140. It combines Byzantine mosaics, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> basilica plan and a painted wooden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muqarnas">muqarnas</a> ceiling by Islamic craftsmen, reflecting the mixed culture of the Norman Kingdom of Sicily.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palatine-chapel-palermo.jpg" alt="Palatine Chapel, Palermo" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urban · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chapelle_Palatine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its monuments, among them the <b>Palatine Chapel</b> with its Byzantine mosaics and Islamic painted ceiling, Cefalù Cathedral and the cathedral of Monreale begun by William II in 1174, are now inscribed together as a World Heritage Site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
